==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|mālōn}}, {{m|goh|mālēn}}, denominative of {{m|goh|māl}} ‘spot, stain’, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*mēlan}} (compare Dutch {{term|maal|lang=nl}}), from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*melh₂-}} ‘dark color’ (compare Welsh {{term|melyn|lang=cy}} ‘yellow’, Lithuanian {{term|melas|mė́las|lang=lt}} ‘black’, Ancient Greek {{term|μέλας|lang=grc}} ‘dark-colored, black’).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈmaːlən/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-malen.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}
* {{rhymes|aːlən|lang=de}}
* {{homophones|mahlen|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-weak|malt|malte|gemalt}}

# to [[paint]]

====Conjugation====
{{de-conj-weak|mal|gemalt|h}}

====Synonyms====
* [[streichen]]

====Related terms====
* [[Maler]]
* [[Malerei]]
* [[Gemälde]]
* [[Bild]]

====Descendants====
* Russian: {{l|ru|малевать}}
* Ukrainian: {{l|uk|малювати}}

